                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Competitive inhibition is a type of reversible enzymatic inhibition in which the inhibitor is competing with the substrate. When the medication is fully metabolised, there should be no further effects\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Non-competitive inhibition is a type of reversible enzymatic inhibition that involves the drug binding to the enzyme and changing the shape of the enzyme so that the natural substrate does not bind.</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect, irreversible enzymatic inhibitors normally bind covalently (share electrons), so their actions remain longer than the half life would predict. But we have ways of degrading the drug at the site, and if that is not possible, or if that takes too long, the cell will produce more of that enzyme.</div>\n",
                    "tip": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed, irreversible enzymatic inhibitors normally bind covalently (share electrons), so their actions remain longer than the half life would predict. But we have ways of degrading the drug at the site, and if that is not possible, or if it takes too long, the cell will produce more of that enzyme.</div>\n"
                  },
                  "text": "Irreversible enzymatic inhibition destroys the ability of the cell to produce that enzyme\n"
                }
              ],
